From 997eb89a08e536d7c78fea902d6ec631dd82c191 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:59:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoRenderers, windowing/gbm: zoom, vertical shift and
 stretching keep native Dolby Vision

Any change of the view (zoom, a vertical shift out of the raster, stretching) and any stream whose
pixels are not square took the picture off native Dolby Vision output, to HDR10 (CRendererVAAPIGLES::
DVGeometry(), and the player information said "the view settings (zoom, stretch or shift)"). Users ask
for zoom to fill 2.39:1 on big televisions and for the vertical shift with projectors.

Now the geometry carries the part of the picture that Kodi shows, drawn where Kodi draws it
(DVBRIDGE::ViewGeometry(), DVViewGeometry.h): Kodi's own source and destination rectangles, which
CBaseRenderer::ManageRenderArea() has already cut to the screen; the place put on whole pixels of the
raster with even columns (the 4:2:2 transport), and the part of the picture moved with each edge by
Kodi's map from the destination to the source, so that what is drawn is what Kodi would draw there. The
bridge draws it in the source transport, scaled in the composer's representation, or converted, with the
level 5 area through the view. The whole picture at its own shape inside the raster takes the geometry it
took, without a view: the same frames, the same bytes. A frame drawn with another view is another frame
for the repeat of an unchanged picture on the screen (DVBRIDGE::SameGeometry()): a zoom changed in a pause
draws again.

Tested on the host (viewgeom_test.cpp): zoom of 4K and of 1080p, a shift up out of the raster, edges
between pixels, a 1440x1080 stretched to 16:9, nothing on the raster; the geometries with views drawn by
the bridge: its commit. Not seen on a display.
